00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.470 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  fine wood furniture is a fixture of  homes throughout the world but getting  the raw material to make it doesn't  always mean  raiding our forests for some  manufacturers it's now coming from  urban areas where downed trees and old  buildings might otherwise end up in  landfills  and along with helping the environment  it is also creating jobs in  neighborhoods that need them errol  barnett has more  at this baltimore warehouse the lumber  is anything but run-of-the-mill  a lot of this lumber was harvested  around the civil war and a lot of the  the trees that were turned into this  lumber really started growing probably  in the 1500s  the antique yellow pine was saved from  crumbling row homes like these  so it has a grain that you wouldn't find  today it has a color that you wouldn't  find today  and it really just as importantly to us  it has a story  and we can be able to kind of hold on to  that little bit of history  max pollock is the founder of brick and  board a company that sells  reclaimed wood and other salvage  material in baltimore  a city with an estimated sixteen  thousand vacant and abandoned properties  for five years you've been a part of  what people describe as the urban  lumber movement what is it and how do  you see  yourself fitting into it it refers to  both lumber that's harvested from  buildings that would otherwise be  discarded and sent to a landfill and it  also refers to  capturing material that falls during  storms woodworkers across the country  are building the movement  but pollock says there's more work to be  done if you were to pull  100 people who are working in the  reclaimed wood field the truth is is  that a lot of them look like me they're  white guys with beards and with flannel  shirts but there really isn't any reason  why that should be the case it's always  been important for us to  try to democratize these skills a little  bit um to try to take people who may not  have had  the opportunity to go to shop class or  may not have had the opportunity  to apprentice with the carpenter and  teach them about the material teach them  about milling this stuff  so that they can create a career out of  it like 21 year old dante godwin who's  worked here for about a year and a half  he appreciates the opportunity he tells  me in a city with an unemployment rate  that usually tracks a few points higher  than the national average  it's not that easy getting opportunities  i was lucky i had mentors to give me i  wouldn't knew about this opportunity  if it weren't for them so even in  baltimore  with a high black population and a rich  history  it's very difficult for young black men  to get started  yes most definitely that's why more  black man is in the streets than having  a job  brick and board is just one of the  private partners in the urban wood  project  a collaboration between the city of  baltimore the usda forest service and  others  the hope is that salvaging wood can  revitalize the city  this used to be abandoned row homes  it's now a park exactly right it's a  park you can't hear these birds and  residents comment on that  co-leader of the urban wood project is  sarah hines of the usda forest service  she wants to help other cities replicate  what she calls  an urban wood economy zip codes like  this and similar have  high rates of unemployment high rates of  poverty  high rates of incarceration underlying  health issues  this project can ameliorate a lot of  that by  thinking about ways to employ people  give people opportunities  avoid waste and plant trees in places  that desperately  need them i don't think most people  realize that  logs in a landfill are actually a bad  thing yeah if you think about a tree  it's sequestering or capturing carbon so  that's co2 that's in the air  and it stores it in the body of the tree  and so when that tree is removed and  it's decomposing it releases all of that  co2  back into the atmosphere contributing  obviously to climate change  jennifer seliga leads the urban wood  rescue in sacramento which is funded in  part by a grant by the california  department of forestry and fire  protection  she says her organization has  sequestered six thousand  seven hundred and seventy metric tons of  carbon  that's equal to about seven hundred  thirty one thousand gallons of gas  we're able to divert logs that would  normally go to the landfill  into our yard and then get them back out  into the community  but seliga admits this process isn't  easy  it's a long endeavor from when the log  is taken down to when it becomes a piece  of furniture  and it can be very costly building out  partnerships and building that community  is just really important to success  one of those partnerships is with  minnesota-based room and board  a furniture company that is also part of  the urban wood project  turning reclaimed wood from baltimore  sacramento detroit and minneapolis  into sustainable furniture and a core we  have one planet  so all of us need to look at not only as  leaders but as individuals to say how  can we make a difference  room and board president and ceo bruce  champeau says  his company has an environmental and  social responsibility  it's a combination of using those  materials and creating jobs for people  that didn't have an opportunity so it's  more than just about  role resources absolutely the social  aspect of it  is equally as important and i think when  you take those two things and you  combine them  that's where it becomes something really  magical remodeling the way we care  for each other for cbs this morning  saturday  errol barnett baltimore that's that  both ends it looks great it looks great  it helps the environment and it helps  people you know i've gone through  many abandoned houses and it was the  thing that i said to myself  where is all of this material going and  so now  there's a place for it to go i'm just so  excited about this great story  vidya and ariel errol just great story

